    "result_text": "There's only one runnable test class (`AcceptanceTest`); the other two are support classes. The full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change makes locality derivation prefer the postcode over the city.\n\n**What I changed** \u2014 `OwnerMapper.java`:\n- Added a `REGION_POSTCODES` table (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099).\n- Added a private helper `regionFromPostcode(String)` that returns the region whose inclusive range contains the postcode, or `null` when the postcode is absent, non-numeric, or in no known range.\n- Rewrote `locality(Owner)` to try the postcode first and only fall back to the existing `CITY_REGION` city table (else `\"UNKNOWN\"`) when the postcode doesn't resolve.\n\n**The subtle bug I hit and fixed:** I first wrote `regionFromPostcode` as a `default` interface method. MapStruct treats any single-arg `String\u2192String` method as an implicit mapping/conversion method, so it silently wrapped *every* String field in `toOwner`/`toOwnerDto` with it \u2014 blanking `firstName`, `city`, `telephone`, etc., which surfaced as a 500 `ConstraintViolationException` (`must not be empty`) at persist time. Marking the helper `private` keeps MapStruct from picking it up while leaving the logic intact.\n\nKnown cities still map to the same region (they carry in-range postcodes), and a city sharing a name is now disambiguated by its postcode \u2014 e.g. an unknown city with postcode `3000` resolves to `VIC`. All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
